 +section('four-week-answer','Worked example: $13 an hour vs $10 an hour over 4 weeks',table(['','Job A','Job B'],[['Hourly rate','$13','$10'],['Hours per week','15','18'],['Weekly pay','$195','$180'],['Pay over 4 weeks','$780','$720'],['Annual pay (52 weeks)','$10,140','$9,360']],'Gross figures. Both jobs are compared over the same 4-week window.')+para('Job A wins by $60 over four weeks — $780 against $720 — even though Job B works three more hours a week. The method is the same as in the example above: multiply rate by weekly hours, and compare both jobs over the same number of weeks. Here the extra hours do not save Job B, because its rate is $3 an hour lower: $15 less every week, $60 less over four weeks, $780 less over a year.')+para('To run your own two offers through the calculator above, first annualize each hourly offer the same way: Job A is $195 × 52 = $10,140 a year at 15 hours a week, and Job B is $180 × 52 = $9,360 a year at 18 hours a week. Enter those salaries with their weekly hours (bonus $0, paid time off 0 weeks, commute 0 hours) and the calculator gives effective rates of $13.00 and $10.00 per committed hour — Job A ahead by $3.00 per committed hour. Then add your real bonus, paid time off, and commute to see the full comparison. If you need the annual figure for any other hourly rate first, use the <a href="/hourly-to-salary-calculator/">hourly to salary calculator</a>.'))+section('commute','What a commute really costs',table(['Weekly commute','Hours per year','Value at $25/hour'],[['2.5 hours','125 hours','$3,125'],['5 hours','250 hours','$6,250'],['7.5 hours','375 hours','$9,375'],['10 hours','500 hours','$12,500']],'50 working weeks. The dollar column values commute time at a $25 hourly rate to show its scale; your own effective rate gives your personal figure.')+para('A one-hour daily round-trip commute adds up to about 250 hours a year — more than six full work weeks. A remote or nearby job can quietly be worth thousands more than a higher-paying job across town.'))
