 const freelanceFaq=[
 ['What counts as a billable hour?','An hour that a client pays for. Leave room for admin, proposals, marketing, and other work that does not generate a direct charge. Many freelancers bill only half to two-thirds of their working time.'],
 ['Is the tax reserve a tax estimate?','No. You enter your own reserve percentage as a planning assumption. Actual tax depends on your circumstances and jurisdiction. The tool does not calculate it.'],
 ['Why is a freelance rate so much higher than an employee wage?','An employee wage comes with employer-paid benefits, payroll tax contributions, paid leave, and equipment. A freelancer funds all of that from the rate, and only gets paid for billable hours — so the rate has to cover far more than the paycheck alone.'],
 ['How many billable hours should I plan for?','Start from your real week. If you work 40 hours and spend 15 on admin, marketing, and communication, 25 billable hours is a realistic plan. Overestimating billable hours is the most common reason a target rate comes out too low.'],
 ['What profit margin should I use?','The margin is the share of revenue kept as business profit after income, the tax reserve, and expenses. Ten to twenty percent is a common planning range. It is a share of revenue, not a markup on costs.'],
 ['Does this rate guarantee I will hit my income goal?','No. It is the rate your plan requires. Whether you earn it depends on winning enough work at that rate. If the market will not support the rate, the honest levers are more billable hours, lower expenses, or a lower income target.'],
 ['What is self-employment tax?','It is the Social Security and Medicare tax a freelancer pays on net earnings: 15.3% applied to 92.35% of net earnings, or about 14.13% effective, before income tax. That is why a 25–30% tax reserve is a common planning range.']
 ];
