 section('formula','How is a freelance rate calculated?',`<div class="formula-card"><div class="formula">Target revenue = [income goal ÷ (1 − tax reserve) + expenses] ÷ (1 − profit margin)</div><p class="example">[$60,000 ÷ 0.75 + $6,000] ÷ 0.90 = $95,556 of required revenue — then ÷ 1,200 billable hours = $79.63/hour</p></div>`+para('The math works backwards from your life, not forwards from a market guess. First your take-home goal is increased to leave room for tax. Expenses are added. The total is increased again so profit remains after everything else is paid. Only then is it divided by the hours clients will actually pay for.'))
 +section('billable','Billable hours decide the rate',table(['Billable hours / week','Billable hours / year','Required rate'],[['15','720','$132.72'],['20','960','$99.54'],['25','1,200','$79.63'],['30','1,440','$66.36'],['35','1,680','$56.88'],['40','1,920','$49.77']],'Same $95,556 revenue target at 48 billable weeks. Halving your billable hours doubles the rate you need — which is why realistic hours matter more than any other input.')+para('Admin, proposals, marketing, invoicing, and learning are real work that no client pays for directly. If you work 40 hours a week but can only bill 25 of them, plan on 25. A rate built on hours you cannot bill will miss your income goal even if every client says yes.'))
 +section('employee','Why a freelance rate is not an employee wage',para('A $60,000 salary equals about $28.85 an hour — yet the same $60,000 take-home goal needs a $79.63 freelance rate in this example. The gap is not greed; it is everything employment normally covers: employer payroll taxes, health insurance, retirement contributions, paid vacation and sick days, equipment, software, and the unpaid hours between projects. Your rate has to fund all of it.')+para('Comparing a freelance rate to a salary hourly equivalent without those costs is the most common pricing mistake new freelancers make. Use the <a href="/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">salary to hourly calculator</a> to see the employee side of the comparison.'))
 +section('reserve','Tax reserve and profit margin',para('The tax reserve is a planning set-aside, not a tax calculation: at 25%, the model keeps $20,000 of the $80,000 grossed-up income aside so $60,000 remains. Your real reserve depends on your tax situation — many freelancers set aside 25–30%. The profit margin is different from profit on a single job: it is the share of all revenue the business keeps, which funds slow months, new equipment, and growth.')+para('Self-employment tax is the reason the reserve runs high: it is 15.3% on 92.35% of net self-employment earnings (about 14.13% effective) before income tax. A 25–30% reserve covers that plus federal and state income tax for many freelancers. The calculator also shows your day rate — the hourly rate × 8 — for day-based quotes.'))
 +section('related','Related calculators',related('freelance-rate'))
 +section('tool-faqs','Frequently asked questions',faqHtml(freelanceFaq))
